after.evidence:
Scaled RATTLE equations have unique local multiplier branches: The regularity, nonzero denominator, spectral gap, or explicit failure clause relevant to degeneracy is stated in Statement: Let $C$ be a compact subset of the regular phase manifold whose configuration projection lies in a compact regular chart. For each $(q,p)\in C$, the two scaled RATTLE equations in [[def-regular-classical-rattle-step-map]] have unique local roots in neighborhoods of
$$\lambda_0=\mu_0= rac12A(q)^{-1} igl(D^2c(q)[v,v]-J(q)M^{-1}
abla V(q) igr),\qquad v=M^{-1}p.$$
In local phase coordinates the position root depends smoothly on $(h,q,p)$, and the momentum root depends smoothly on $(h,q,p,\lambda)$ when the position multiplier is treated as a parameter near its selected root. The local branches agree on overlaps after shrinking their neighborhoods. A finite cover of $C$ therefore gives a common $h_0(C)>0$ for $|h|<h_0(C)$; the derivatives of each fixed finite order are uniformly bounded on this cover, and the two multiplier Jacobians remain uniformly invertible there. The exact position roots can also be kept in an ambient neighborhood of the compact regular configuration projection where $J$ has full row rank, so their constrained positions remain in $Q_{\mathrm{reg}}$. At $h=0$ both Jacobians equal $-A(q)$. The existing argument records step 1.1: Put $v=M^{-1}p$ and $w(\lambda)=-M^{-1}( rac12
abla V(q)+J(q)^T\lambda)$. Then $q_1=q+hv+h^2w(\lambda)$. Since $c(q)=0$ and $J(q)v=0$, [F3] extends $h^{-2}c(q_1)$ smoothly, with value $F_q(0,q,p,\lambda)= rac12D^2c(q)[v,v]- rac12J(q)M^{-1}
abla V(q)-A(q)\lambda$. Its $\lambda$ derivative is $-A(q)$, which is invertible. Since $A$ is smooth and invertible, $A^{-1}=\operatorname{adj}(A)/\det A$ is smooth locally, so the center $\lambda_0$ varies smoothly with the phase input. Thus [F4] gives the unique local position multiplier branch at that center. [F1, F2, F3, F4, algebra]; step 3.1: The branch equations are smooth in local phase coordinates, and $A^{-1}$ is uniformly bounded on the compact configuration projection of $C$. Since $J$ has full row rank on that compact projection, continuity gives an ambient neighborhood where it remains full row rank. Around each point of $C$, shrink the phase and multiplier neighborhoods so both roots remain in common IFT uniqueness neighborhoods on overlaps and exact $q_1$ remains in this regular neighborhood. These neighborhoods cover $C$; compactness gives a finite subcover and the minimum of their positive step radii is a common $h_0(C)$. The smooth branch derivatives are bounded on smaller compact neighborhoods covering $C$ for $|h|\le h_0(C)$, and continuity of the multiplier Jacobians preserves uniform invertibility after a further shrink. [F2, F4, step 1.1, step 2.1, choose] ∎

